  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA pleasant and cool summer day continues with quiet weather \ncontinuing right through Monday, although MOnday should be a \ntouch warmer with temperatures climbing into the low 80s. A \nshortwave approaches this afternoon which may squeeze a few \ndrops out of the developing cumulus field along the \nInternational Border, but otherwise the dry air in place will \nhold steady. Temperatures tonight should once again fall quickly\nas mostly clear skies and calm winds allow for efficient \nradiational cooling. I would expect another night with \nwidespread low 50s, with mid to upper 40s in the usual better \nradiating locations and have blended in some cooler MOS guidance\nto the NBM to reflect that.\n\nA cold front begins moving eastward toward the region Monday, \nbut it doesn't look like it is going to make much progress. The \n26/12Z CAMs all depict any prefrontal showers losing the battle \nto the dry air that will remain overhead. Most of the area is \ngoing to end up dry, but I could see a few showers making it \ninto southwestern New Hampshire tomorrow afternoon and evening. \nAn isolated rumble of thunder is not out of the question either \nas daytime heating and slowly increasing dewpoints create just \nenough instability. Clouds move in Monday night ahead of the \nnext system, so nighttime temperatures are going to end up \nwarmer than the previous nights, only dropping into the mid- to \nupper 50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nClosed low moves towards southern New England Tuesday. The \nupper trough is deep, and will gradually work towards cutting \noff through late week. For Tuesday, this will force the \ndevelopment of showers with a few thunderstorms through the \nafternoon and early evening mostly across New Hampshire. Heavy \nprecip parameters aren't excessive, but flow is weak. This could\nresult in stronger showers or storms featuring slow storm \nmotion if updrafts can persist. Morning RRFS runs do depict some\npoints of enhanced QPF through the afternoon hours across NH, \nbut these remain fairly isolated as they eventually rain \nthemselves out. Coverage of these heavier showers is expected to\nlessen through the early evening hours.\n\nProgressing into midweek, more widespread rainfall is likely \nWed/Thurs. Trends have been somewhat stubborn for this system, \nbut closed lows are often a scenario that sees greater than \nnormal uncertainty in the medium range. The incoming low will \ntend to deform a pool of moisture settled across the eastern \nCONUS, curling moisture transport SW to SE off the New England \ncoast. Low level wind field associated with the low is marginal \nbut broad while the upper level support is lackluster. For \nmoisture, IVT isn't overly impressive (with SE directions not \nfavorable for New England ARs) as likely probabilities for \ngreater than 500 kg/m/s remains offshore and just noses into \nsouthern NH/coastal southern ME briefly Wed afternoon. \n\nContinue to not be overly concerned about short-fused flash \nflooding this week given the antecedent dry conditions. However,\nwill monitor any localized heavy rainfall locations on Tuesday,\nto possibly be exacerbated Wednesday/Thursday amid the forecast\nbroader stratiform precipitation. While the greatest \nprecipitation is expected Wednesday and Thursday, dreary \nconditions with showers could persist through Friday, with \nreinforced forcing arriving for the weekend with continued \nprecip chances.\n\nThe rain mid week will tend to bring a period of more mild \ntemperatures to the area given additional cloud cover and cooler\nairmass. The northern fringe, roughly the northern third of the\nforecast area, could see warmer temperatures in the mid 70s to \naround 80 through this period if clouds are thinner.",
